## Session summary

**Criteria NOT met** — `cache_coherency` still fails, so no marker written. We are not ready to ship.

### Two real fixes landed (build `B5C1FA0F`, deployed test1-4 — both KEEP)

1. **Root-caused & fixed: the cross-node eviction-ring never delivered under CAW.** The INODE_FREE (sess55) + DIR_MODIFY (sess80) coherency signals ride the disklock heartbeat, but `mxfs_disklock_monitor_node` was only wired into the TCP path — the CAW path (`v5_mount.c`) never called it, so the HB consumer skipped *every* peer slot. Proven with new markers: **producer `P-EVICT-STAGE` fired plentifully, consumer `P-EVICT-DISPATCH` = 0 on every node.** Fixed with a self-healing auto-monitor in `disklock_hb_fn` (auto-monitor any active peer slot). After fix: dispatch ~200/node, DIR_MODIFY gate-passes 34-52/node. **These signals had been dead cross-node the entire project.**

2. **Defensive `REMOVE-REVALIDATE` in `xfs_remove`** (`xfs_dir_lookup_locked` pre-validation before the transaction is dirtied) → converts the catastrophic `xfs_trans_cancel`-dirty **SHUTDOWN** into a benign "rm: No such file". **Proven: 0 shutdowns / 0 trans_cancel across a 3-iter run** (was stochastic node-death that also killed `cross_write_read`).

### Decisive diagnostic progress
- **Unified the two failure faces**: the bnobt-family SHUTDOWN trace is `xfs_dir_removename rc=-2 (ENOENT)` — a node deleting its **own** just-created file whose dirent is **durably missing from the on-disk shared dir block**. Both the soft "peer sees stale file" and the shutdown are the same **durable dir-block lost-update** under concurrent multi-node shared-dir modify.
- **Key insight**: the auto-monitor fix *exposes* the lost-update — nodes now actually re-read the (corrupt) disk and lose their **own** created files (90/120 visible). The corruption is on the platter; not re-reading merely hid it. So the true root is the **write-side durable lost-update during concurrent shared-dir CREATE**.

### Tried and reverted (don't repeat)
Per-unlink durability barrier in `xfs_remove`: whole-AG `xfs_ail_push_ag_sync` wedged 3 nodes; targeted single-buffer `xfs_bwrite` needs `log_force` first (else `wait_unpin` hangs); with log_force it regressed 2→31 by writing a **torn transaction** (new dir block, old inode) to disk.

### Next session (concrete)
Consulted Gemini (RULE 5) — hypothesis is an EX-handoff durability-ordering gap (in_AIL buffers), but markers `DIR-STALE-SKIP=0` / `P-EVICT-SKIP=0` show that specific read-side path isn't firing. **Next: run a concurrent-CREATE-only test and FUA-compare on-disk vs in-core dirent counts** — if disk < 120, the write-side lost-update is confirmed as root; then fix EX-handoff durability ordering in the BAST-release drain worker (targeted AIL-detach, NOT whole-AIL sync). Recorded in `sess82_lessons.md` + MEMORY.md.

(Note: a 3-iter verification run was still finishing in the background at the relay boundary; iter1 = 31 soft fails / no shutdown, iter2 = slow-timeout / no shutdown.)

